ODFileSystem for AROS
====================

The source/ directory is a Git submodule of
https://github.com/reinauer/ODFileSystem.git, pinned to a specific commit.
The external filesystem retains its BSD-2-Clause license; see
source/LICENSE. The build wrapper and module configuration belong to AROS.

Initialize the source before configuring or building AROS:

    git submodule update --init rom/filesys/odfs/source

Build the resident, disk-loadable handler:

    make kernel-fs-odfs

The result is L/ODFileSystem-handler. This is an optional handler: the
normal build dependencies, boot packages and existing DOSDrivers continue
to use the existing filesystems. This module registers only CD01 and its
own ODFileSystem resident name, without legacy filesystem aliases.

To try it, build the target above and copy ODF0 to Storage/DOSDrivers.
Adjust Device and Unit in that file for the drive or image device, then
mount it explicitly. Choose an unused DOS device name and avoid mounting
the same medium through two handlers at once. The example's 32768-byte
stack request is conservative, not a measured minimum.

Keep the upstream revision pinned for reproducible builds. Contributions
back to ODFileSystem are encouraged so fixes can be shared across targets.
The BSD-2-Clause license also permits AROS to modify and maintain its own
copy, subject to the license's notice requirements.

ODFileSystem supports ISO 9660/High Sierra, Rock Ridge, Joliet, UDF, HFS,
HFS+, multisession data discs, and audio tracks as WAV or AIFF files.
It is read-only. Control supports LOWERCASE, NOROCKRIDGE, NOJOLIET,
HFSFIRST, UDF, AIFF, FILEBUFFERS and METACACHE; see source/README.md.

Run the portable parser/cache unit tests with:

    make test-filesys-odfs

See source/docs/testing.md for format fixtures, malformed images, fuzzing,
and source/tests/aros/check_abi.sh for isolated x86_64 AROS packet-layout
checks. The Linux packet harness makes no Exec/DOS calls. Successful
cross-builds and host tests do not prove AROS device I/O, media-change
interrupts, or boot behavior on any target.
